<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Proof of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3 EM-NeSy Learning ‣ EM-NeSy: Expectation Maximization for Neurosymbolic Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a></h2>

<div id="A1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that, by the chain rule, we have</span></p>
<table id="A1.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\log f(\boldsymbol{\theta})=\frac{\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}f(\boldsymbol{\theta})}{f(\boldsymbol{\theta})}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\log f(\boldsymbol{\theta})=\frac{\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}f(\boldsymbol{\theta})}{f(\boldsymbol{\theta})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text">for any differentiable function <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, the claim follows from the following sequence of equalities:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta})\overset{\eqref{std_loss}}{=}-\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\log p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.700em">(</mtext><mtext mathsize="0.700em"><a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2.2 Probabilistic NeSy Learning as a Latent Variable Model ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ EM-NeSy: Expectation Maximization for Neurosymbolic Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a></mtext><mtext mathsize="0.700em">)</mtext></mrow></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\theta})\overset{\eqref{std_loss}}{=}-\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\log p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{\eqref{log_chain_rule}}{=}-\frac{\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}{p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.700em">(</mtext><mtext mathsize="0.700em"><a href="#A1.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Proof. ‣ Appendix A Proof of ‣ EM-NeSy: Expectation Maximization for Neurosymbolic Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a></mtext><mtext mathsize="0.700em">)</mtext></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{\eqref{log_chain_rule}}{=}-\frac{\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}{p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{\eqref{std_loss}}{=}-\frac{\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\sum_{\mathbf{z}\in R(\mathbf{Z})}p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})p_{\text{Sy}}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{z})}{p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.700em">(</mtext><mtext mathsize="0.700em"><a href="#S2.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 2.2 Probabilistic NeSy Learning as a Latent Variable Model ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ EM-NeSy: Expectation Maximization for Neurosymbolic Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a></mtext><mtext mathsize="0.700em">)</mtext></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>Ne</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>Sy</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{\eqref{std_loss}}{=}-\frac{\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\sum_{\mathbf{z}\in R(\mathbf{Z})}p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})p_{\text{Sy}}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{z})}{p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\frac{\sum_{\mathbf{z}\in R(\mathbf{Z})}p_{\text{Sy}}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{z})\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}{p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>Sy</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>Ne</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\frac{\sum_{\mathbf{z}\in R(\mathbf{Z})}p_{\text{Sy}}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{z})\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}{p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{\eqref{log_chain_rule}}{=}-\frac{\sum_{\mathbf{z}\in R(\mathbf{Z})}p_{\text{Sy}}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{z})p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\log p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}{p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.700em">(</mtext><mtext mathsize="0.700em"><a href="#A1.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ Proof. ‣ Appendix A Proof of ‣ EM-NeSy: Expectation Maximization for Neurosymbolic Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a></mtext><mtext mathsize="0.700em">)</mtext></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>Sy</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>Ne</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>Ne</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{\eqref{log_chain_rule}}{=}-\frac{\sum_{\mathbf{z}\in R(\mathbf{Z})}p_{\text{Sy}}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{z})p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\log p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}{p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{\eqref{eq_NeSy_pred}}{=}-\frac{\sum_{\mathbf{z}\in R(\mathbf{Z})}p(\mathbf{z},\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\log p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}{p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.700em">(</mtext><mtext mathsize="0.700em"><a href="#S2.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 2.2 Probabilistic NeSy Learning as a Latent Variable Model ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ EM-NeSy: Expectation Maximization for Neurosymbolic Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></mtext><mtext mathsize="0.700em">)</mtext></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>Ne</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{\eqref{eq_NeSy_pred}}{=}-\frac{\sum_{\mathbf{z}\in R(\mathbf{Z})}p(\mathbf{z},\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\log p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}{p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{\eqref{eq_latent_posterior}}{=}-\frac{\sum_{\mathbf{z}\in R(\mathbf{Z})}p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})p(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\log p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}{p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.700em">(</mtext><mtext mathsize="0.700em"><a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 3 EM-NeSy Learning ‣ EM-NeSy: Expectation Maximization for Neurosymbolic Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a></mtext><mtext mathsize="0.700em">)</mtext></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>Ne</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{\eqref{eq_latent_posterior}}{=}-\frac{\sum_{\mathbf{z}\in R(\mathbf{Z})}p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})p(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\log p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}{p(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\sum_{\mathbf{z}\in R(\mathbf{Z})}p(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\log p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>Ne</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\sum_{\mathbf{z}\in R(\mathbf{Z})}p(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\log p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{\eqref{pseudo_label}}{=}-\sum_{\mathbf{z}\in R(\mathbf{Z})}\ell(\mathbf{z})\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\log p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext mathsize="0.700em">(</mtext><mtext mathsize="0.700em"><a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3 EM-NeSy Learning ‣ EM-NeSy: Expectation Maximization for Neurosymbolic Learning" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a></mtext><mtext mathsize="0.700em">)</mtext></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>Ne</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{\eqref{pseudo_label}}{=}-\sum_{\mathbf{z}\in R(\mathbf{Z})}\ell(\mathbf{z})\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\log p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{EM-NeSy}}(\theta)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>EM-NeSy</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{EM-NeSy}}(\theta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Multi-M EM</h2>

<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> One training step of Multi-M EM</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
            
            
           training sample <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y})</annotation></semantics></math>, number of <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> steps <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
           Compute <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>Ne</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{Ne}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
           Compute <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{Sy}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>Sy</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{Sy}}(\mathbf{z}|\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x},\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            
            
           For <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>: Backpropagate w.r.t. <math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{EM-NeSy}}(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>EM-NeSy</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{EM-NeSy}}(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Experimental details</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments were performed using a single NVIDIA L40S GPU with 48 GB of memory on a machine equipped with a 128-thread CPU and 256 GB of RAM.
For all experiments, we report the mean and standard deviation of the accuracies over random seeds <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0,\dots,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0,\dots,4</annotation></semantics></math>.
We compute training times by</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Benchmarks</h3>

<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MNISTAdd</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MNIST dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> contains <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>60,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> training and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> test examples. We further split the original training set into a training and a validation split of sizes <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. From each of the resulting three MNIST splits and for a given number of digits <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct MNISTAdd datasets of size <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{50{,}000}{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>50,000</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{50{,}000}{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each MNIST digit is used in at most one example, where every example consists of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math> MNIST images representing two <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-digit numbers, and the corresponding label is their sum.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Visual Sudoku</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The predefined Visual Sudoku datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> consist of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500</annotation></semantics></math> Sudoku grids of size either <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\times 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times 4</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9\times 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9\times 9</annotation></semantics></math>, split into <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> training examples, <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> validation examples, and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> test examples.
A total of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> splits are provided. We use the first one for all our experiments.
Within each split, there is no overlap between the MNIST images used in the training, validation, and test sets.
All training puzzles are valid Sudokus, whereas in the validation and test sets half of the puzzles are invalid.
Invalid puzzles are generated by corrupting valid ones, either by replacing a correct digit with an incorrect one or by swapping two valid digits.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Warcraft path-planning</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The predefined Warcraft path-planning datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> consist of grid-based maps of size <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12\times 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12\times 12</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\times 30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\times 30</annotation></semantics></math>. The maps are generated using a custom random sampling procedure based on <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="142" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>142</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">142</annotation></semantics></math> terrain tiles from the <em id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Warcraft II</em> game environment. Each tile belongs to one of five terrain types, each associated with a fixed traversal cost ranging from <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.8</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.2</annotation></semantics></math>. For each map, the supervision is given as a <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12\times 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12\times 12</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\times 30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\times 30</annotation></semantics></math> binary matrix indicating the shortest path from the top-left to the bottom-right corner. The training, validation, and test splits contain <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}000</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> examples, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Neural component</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For MNIST-digit recognition, we use a LeNet-style convolutional neural network <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> consisting of two convolution–max-pooling blocks with <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 5</annotation></semantics></math> kernels and ReLU activations, followed by three fully connected layers.
Given inputs of size <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28\times 28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>28</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28\times 28</annotation></semantics></math>, the network produces <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional class probabilities for each digit.
For inputs containing multiple digits, the same network is applied independently to each digit via weight sharing.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To extract the latent costs of the terrain tiles in the Warcraft path-finding task, we use the first five layers of ResNet18 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> followed by a max-pooling operation. The resulting continuous value for each grid cell is then mapped to a probability distribution over the five terrain types by computing the <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> distances to learnable class centers (initialized with <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0,1.0,2.0,3.0,4.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0,1.0,2.0,3.0,4.0</annotation></semantics></math>) and applying a softmax transformation with temperature <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Symbolic component</h3>

<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Belief propagation</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use our own implementation of Pearl’s loopy belief propagation algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
It employs a synchronized message<span id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>passing scheme in which all messages are updated in parallel.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For MNISTAdd, the underlying factor graph contains <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math> categorical variables for each input digit and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> categorical variables for each sum digit, as well as <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> binary carry variables.
The resulting graph is a tree, so belief propagation yields exact marginals.
We perform <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math> iterations of belief propagation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For Visual Sudoku, the factor graph contains <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> categorical variables for (<math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{4,9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{4,9\}</annotation></semantics></math>) for every cell of the Sudoku grid and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2\cdot n\cdot\binom{n}{2}+\frac{n^{2}\cdot(\sqrt{n}-1)}{2}\in\{56,810\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>56,810</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2\cdot n\cdot\binom{n}{2}+\frac{n^{2}\cdot(\sqrt{n}-1)}{2}\in\{56,810\}</annotation></semantics></math> binary variables for each of the <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> pairs of cells that share a row, column, or box.
In addition, <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m-1</annotation></semantics></math> binary variables encode constraints that combine subsets of <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> pair constraints. We perform <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> iterations of belief propagation, using a damping parameter of <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math> for the message updates. To avoid numerical degeneracies in deterministic factors where message entries may collapse to zero we apply a clamping parameter of <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ABC rejection sampling</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both in the Visual Sudoku and Warcraft path-planning task, we follow the approach described in <a href="#S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" title="ABC rejection sampling. ‣ 4.2 Sampling-based inference ‣ 4 Generalization to alternative inference schemes ‣ EM-NeSy: Expectation Maximization for Neurosymbolic Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. We sample uniformly from the neurally predicted distribution <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\text{Ne}}(\cdot|x,\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>Ne</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{Ne}}(\cdot|x,\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> for the Sudoku digits or Warcraft grid cells, which factorizes into the distributions for the single digits/grid cells <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the Visual Sudoku experiment, let <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the constraint <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}\neq Z_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}\neq Z_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As described in <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 4.1. ‣ ABC rejection sampling. ‣ 4.2 Sampling-based inference ‣ 4 Generalization to alternative inference schemes ‣ EM-NeSy: Expectation Maximization for Neurosymbolic Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">example</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we define</p>
<table id="A3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M:=\{m_{ij}:Z_{i},Z_{j}\text{ share a row column or box in the Sudoku}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext> share a row column or box in the Sudoku</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M:=\{m_{ij}:Z_{i},Z_{j}\text{ share a row column or box in the Sudoku}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">to be the set of Sudoku constraints. For every fixed <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> let</p>
<table id="A3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}:=\{m_{ij}\in M:j\in\{1,\dots,n^{2}\}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}:=\{m_{ij}\in M:j\in\{1,\dots,n^{2}\}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">be the set of all constraints that involve <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We further choose the distance measure <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=L^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the summary statistics <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y})=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Z_{i}}(z_{i})=\frac{k}{|M_{i}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Z_{i}}(z_{i})=\frac{k}{|M_{i}|}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a normalization constant and <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of constraints from <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> that are satisfied by the sample <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then we assign the weight</p>
<table id="A3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{C}\rho\bigl(s_{Z_{i}}(z_{i}),s_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y})\bigr)=\frac{1}{C}\left|\frac{k}{|M_{i}|}-0\right|=\frac{k}{C|M_{i}|}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{C}\rho\bigl(s_{Z_{i}}(z_{i}),s_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y})\bigr)=\frac{1}{C}\left|\frac{k}{|M_{i}|}-0\right|=\frac{k}{C|M_{i}|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">to each sample <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a normalization constant.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the Warcraft experiment, we employ a Dijkstra solver that predicts the shortest path for each sample. We let <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{Z_{i}}(z_{i})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{Z_{i}}(z_{i})=1</annotation></semantics></math> if the predicted shortest path for the sample <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> matches the true shortest path on the grid cell <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. both the predicted and true shortest path contain <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> or they both don’t).
We let <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\mathbf{Y}(\mathbf{y})}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\mathbf{Y}(\mathbf{y})}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and assign the weight <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{C}\rho\bigl(s_{Z_{i}}(z_{i}),s_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>C</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>𝐘</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{C}\rho\bigl(s_{Z_{i}}(z_{i}),s_{\mathbf{Y}}(\mathbf{y})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> to the sample <math id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Hyperparameters</h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used the validation splits for hyperparameter tuning.
At test time, we report results using the checkpoint that achieves the highest validation accuracy.
We employ the Adam optimizer for all experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For MNISTAdd, we use a cosine<span id="A3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>annealing learning<span id="A3.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>rate schedule updated after each epoch, beginning at <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> and decaying to <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We train for <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> epochs with batch sizes of <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> digits, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For Visual Sudoku, we use a cosine<span id="A3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>annealing learning<span id="A3.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>rate schedule updated after each epoch, beginning at <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> and decaying to <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We train with a batchsize of <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> epochs for <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\times 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times 4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9\times 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9\times 9</annotation></semantics></math> Sudokus, respectively.
For ABC sampling, we use <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> samples on <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\times 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times 4</annotation></semantics></math> Sudokus and <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A3.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9\times 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9\times 9</annotation></semantics></math> Sudokus.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the Warcraft path-planning experiment, we use a constant learning<span id="A3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>rate of <math id="A3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\cdot 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\cdot 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> on the <math id="A3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12\times 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12\times 12</annotation></semantics></math> grid and <math id="A3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> on the <math id="A3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\times 30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\times 30</annotation></semantics></math> grid.
We train with a batchsize of <math id="A3.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A3.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> epochs, respectively.
We use an entropy regularization parameter of <math id="A3.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.03</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.03</annotation></semantics></math> for training on the <math id="A3.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12\times 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12\times 12</annotation></semantics></math> grid.
For ABC sampling, we used <math id="A3.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> samples on <math id="A3.SS4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12\times 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12\times 12</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\times 30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\times 30</annotation></semantics></math> grids, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.5 </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="A3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We ran the experiments for A<span id="A3.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>NeSI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>, I<span id="A3.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>MLE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>,
EXAL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> and
Scallop <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> with the hyperparameters specified in the original papers.
For A-NeSI we use the symbolic prediction variant.
For A-NeSI on the Warcraft path-planning benchmark, we were not able to reproduce the results due to high variance and training times, so we cite the accuracies from the original paper and report training time on our machines for a single run.
For EXAL on the Warcraft path-planning benchmark, we report training times and accuracies from the original paper. Note that these experiments were performed on a another machine, namely a Dell XPS 15 (i7, 16GB, 512GB, FHD, GPU).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
